  "findings": [
    "Kafka broker CPU per authorization fell from a median 1.45 ms (1.38-1.49) to 0.53 ms (0.39-0.59); every batched run was below every before run.",
    "The outbox publisher fell from 0.67 to 0.35 ms and the feature consumer from 0.53 to 0.19 ms per authorization; the case consumer, which writes SQLite per event, barely changed (0.42 to 0.34).",
    "Stack CPU per authorization fell from a median 8.21 ms (7.71-8.47) to 6.34 ms (5.34-7.17), about 23%; the highest batched run is below the lowest before run.",
    "All batched runs completed 9,000 or 9,001 of 9,001 with no errors; the before runs dropped 32, 0 and 0.",
    "API, PostgreSQL and scorer CPU did not change consistently; their run-to-run spread (for example the scorer 1.85-2.42 ms) is larger than any effect."
  ],
  "semantics": "Delivery stays at least once. Rows are marked published only after every record in the batch is acknowledged; a failed send rolls the transaction back and the batch is sent again later (test: outboxRowsStayPendingWhenASendFails). Consumers commit only after every message in the batch is applied, so a crash replays at most one batch; both stores already ignore repeated auth IDs (test: fraud/tests/test_stream_commits.py). Per-partition order is kept because the idempotent producer allows at most five in-flight requests.",
  "limits": [
    "one machine with k6 on it",
    "three runs per build at a single rate (600/s); the knee was not re-measured",
    "synthetic workload"
  ]
